### Conflicting evidence (vendor vs independent) and how it was resolved

- **Agentic strength.** Inception's launch post sells Mercury 3 on agentic work (TinyFish reports 50% completion on 50 live Mind2Web tasks, and the vendor chart shows τ³-bench 30.2% and DeepSearchQA 71.3%), while the only independent agentic measurement, Vals AI's Terminal-Bench 4.0 run, scores just 3.03% (#55 of 63). Resolution: the vendor numbers are kept as vendor-reported and the independent Terminal-Bench result is treated as the harder, longer-horizon test; the Tool-use score is anchored on the independent figure with the vendor agentic results credited as supporting-but-not-decisive evidence.
- **Coding.** Vendor DeepSWE 46.3% and an unmapped SWE-Bench Atlas bar of 51.08 sit beside independent Vibe Code Bench 60.93% and Code Migration 39.26%. Resolution: DeepSWE is kept as the primary coding signal because it is a repository-level SWE harness, but the independent Vibe/Code-Migration rows cap the score — the vendor SWE-Bench Atlas bar is explicitly excluded from scoring because its metric scale is not published.
- **Speed claims.** 1,068 tok/s is an Inception-reported figure on NVIDIA hardware; Artificial Analysis has not benchmarked Mercury 3 at all (its Inception coverage is Mercury 2 and Mercury 2.5 only), so no third-party throughput figure exists. Resolution: speed is treated as a verified vendor measurement of throughput, not as a quality proxy, and it does not raise any quality score.
- **Diffusion-specific coherence.** Independent Vals AI evidence is the closest thing to a diffusion-coherence warning: Terminal-Bench 4.0 at 3.03%, HLAB-Vals at 5.00% and Legal Research Bench at 28.85% all come from long, nested agentic and rubric-heavy runs, while short-form knowledge scores (GPQA 89.2%) stay high. This pattern is consistent with variance when a diffusion decoder must maintain coherence across many dependent steps, and it is the reason the Tool-use and Coding scores are held well below the vendor narrative.

### Notes on identity

- Mercury 3 is the **general-purpose diffusion reasoning LLM** of the Inception family: an OpenAI-compatible chat model with tool calling, structured outputs and four reasoning-effort levels. It is the successor to Mercury 2 and Mercury 2.5, not a variant of them (40x Mercury 1 in parameters per the vendor).
- It is **not** Mercury Edit 2, the narrow fill-in-the-middle / next-edit prediction model that serves only the FIM and edit-completions endpoints and has no chat, tool-calling or reasoning surface.
- It is **not** Mercury Decide, the typed decision model that serves only the decisions endpoint and returns probabilities for yes/no, choice, score and extraction questions instead of free-form text.
- Identity split used in this report: vendor-reported launch-chart rows are attributed to Inception; rows sourced from Vals AI, Design Arena, LMSpeed/Benchize, OpenRouter and the Inception docs are labelled by their actual runner.

### Normalized scores (1–100)

- **Tool use: 55/100.** Above the mid band because τ³-bench 30.2%, DeepSearchQA 71.3% and BrowseComp+ 55.18% are reported by the vendor and a partner reports 50% completion on live Mind2Web navigation, with native parallel tool calling documented; capped well below frontier because the independent Terminal-Bench 4.0 run is 3.03%, Terminal-Bench 2.1, GDPval and Claw-Eval are all missing, and long nested agentic loops are exactly where a diffusion decoder loses coherence.
- **Reasoning: 70/100.** GPQA Diamond 89.2% and MRCRv2 75.5% (vendor) put it near the frontier on single-shot knowledge and multi-round long-context reasoning, and IFBench 76.8% is solid; capped by the absence of any HLE, LCR or CritPt figure, by the independent Vals Index of only 43.66% (#32 of 36) and by HLAB-Vals at 5.00% and Terminal-Bench 4.0 at 3.03%.
- **Context window: 95/100.** A verified 1,000,000-token window with 131,072 max output puts it in the top tier (95–100); it does not reach 100 because no independent run demonstrates 98% retrieval at 512K or beyond — the only long-context number, MRCRv2 75.5%, is vendor-reported and its tested window length is not published.
- **Multimodal: 15/100.** Text-only: the Inception model table lists supported format `Text` and Vals AI records image input as unsupported, so the score is the text-only floor.
- **Coding: 58/100.** DeepSWE 46.3% (vendor) and Vibe Code Bench 60.93% (independent) put it in the mid band on application building, but Code Migration 39.26% and Terminal-Bench 4.0 3.03% show repository-scale work is weak, and no SWE-bench Verified, LiveCodeBench or SciCode figure exists.
- **Cost efficiency: 97/100.** $0.10 input / $0.35 output per 1M tokens at the current 50%-off launch price (regular $0.20 / $0.70), cached input $0.01, is one of the cheapest paid tiers evaluated; the 100M free tokens on new API keys are a time-limited allowance with a 1,000-request/minute cap and a separate retention policy for enterprise deployments, so it is not scored as a free model.
- **Overall Score: 58.6/100.** Mean of the five quality dimensions (55 + 70 + 95 + 15 + 58) / 5 = 58.6; best fit is latency-critical chat and real-time sub-agent work with a very large context, not long-horizon autonomous coding.

